{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/specs/2026-11-16-pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph.md; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_commit_graph_test.go'","aggregated_output":"---\nslug: pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-16\ncompleted_at:        # stamped on the transition to status: done\nsupersedes:          # slug of the superseded spec (when applicable)\ndepends_on:          # prerequisites, inline list: other-spec, milestone:<roadmap>/<id>, roadmap:<slug>\nremediates:          # optional explicit links: spec:slug@defect-fix, finding:rva-<16hex>/F1@revert (never inferred from depends_on)\npriority:            # integer >= 0 (lower = higher priority); ordering preference, not a blocker\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface:             # optional: minimal | standard | full (default) — minimal drops Tasks and derivable report sections; `pose specs facts <slug>` derives them\ndelivers: capability:doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nFazer o `pose doctor` recomendar, e o `pose doctor --fix` escrever, o commit-graph do Git com filtros de Bloom de caminhos alterados num projeto cujo histórico é grande o bastante para pesar, e fazer o CI do pose-dist escrevê-lo antes dos gates.\n\n### Business value\nA spec `pose-review-ledger-is-read-once-per-operation` mediu, na primeira chamada `pose_closeout_state` de um servidor MCP no harne8, 1.116 comandos `git log -1 --format=%H -- <path>` somando 81 s: cada um percorre o histórico do pose-dist (3.528 commits) até achar o último commit que tocou o arquivo. Com o commit-graph com caminhos alterados (`git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths`), cada consulta cai de 47 ms para 2 ms, sem mudar a saída do Git, e a primeira chamada cai de 136 s para 59 s. É manutenção do repositório, que o motor não faz sozinho numa leitura de governança; o `doctor` é o lugar onde o POSE já recomenda e aplica remediações seguras.\n\n### Constraints\n- A verificação só lê arquivos do Git; não roda nada que escreva.\n- A correção só escreve sob o diretório de objetos do Git, é idempotente e reversível (apagar o arquivo devolve o estado anterior), como as demais do registro do `doctor --fix`.\n- Uma instalação nova, com histórico pequeno, continua sem passos pendentes.\n\n### Non-goals\n- Escrever o commit-graph durante uma operação de governança.\n- Manter o commit-graph atualizado a cada commit (o `git gc` e o `git maintenance` já o reescrevem preservando os filtros).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: When the project root is a Git repository whose HEAD has at least 500 commits, and Git's own trace of a last-commit lookup by path shows no changed-path Bloom filter consulted, `pose doctor` shall report the check `git.commit-graph` at level `next`, with the command that writes it; the judgement follows whichever commit-graph Git loads (its own object directory or an alternate, a single file or a chain).\n- R2: `pose doctor --fix --yes` shall remediate `git.commit-graph` by running `git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths` in the project root, after which the check shall report `ok`.\n- R3: A repository with fewer than 500 commits on HEAD, or one whose last-commit lookup Git answers from the filters, shall report `ok`; a project root that is not a Git repository, or has no commit on HEAD, shall not report the check; any other failure to read the state shall report `warn` with a bounded description of the failure; when Git configuration keeps it from reading commit-graphs or their changed-path filters, `pose doctor` shall report `git.commit-graph-reads` at `warn`, naming the setting, instead of recommending a rewrite.\n- R4: The pose-dist CI jobs that check out the full history shall write the commit-graph with changed paths before their gates.\n\n### Non-functional\n- A verificação roda três comandos Git de leitura e uma busca do último commit de um arquivo com o trace2 ligado.\n\n### Security\n- A correção roda o Git com argumentos fixos no root do projeto; nenhuma entrada do usuário entra no comando.\n\n### Compatibility\n- Saída do `doctor` ganha um check novo; o formato do relatório não muda.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n- `cli/doctor.go`: o check `git.commit-graph` e a entrada no registro de correções.\n- `cli/doctor_commit_graph.go`: a contagem do histórico e a pergunta ao Git, pelo trace2, se ele usa os filtros.\n- `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: o passo que escreve o commit-graph.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_commit_graph.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_commit_graph_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go\n- modified: .github/workflows/ci.yml\n- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n\nComposto em produção pelo `pose doctor` e pelo `pose doctor --fix`. Check permanente `doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph-integration`, evidenceClass `integration`, required: `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph|TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory|TestDoctorCommitGraphTellsUnbornFromBroken|TestDoctorCommitGraphProbesALiteralPath|TestDoctorCommitGraphNamesDisabledReads' -count=1`.\n\n### API/contract changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Data/storage changes\n- Nenhuma no `.pose/`; a correção escreve o commit-graph sob `.git/objects/info/`.\n\n### Technical risks\n- O evento `bloom` do trace2 é saída de diagnóstico do Git; uma versão que o renomeie faria o check recomendar a correção sempre. A correção é inofensiva, e o teste de integração com o Git real reprova se o evento sumir.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Implementation\n- [x] Check e correção `git.commit-graph` no `doctor` (R1, R2, R3).\n- [x] Passo do CI (R4).\n\n### Validation\n- [x] Testes com repositórios reais acima e abaixo do limite, com e sem filtros, com prova de reprovação.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Date: 2026-11-16\n- Context: o follow-up F1 da spec `pose-review-ledger-is-read-once-per-operation`; o maintainer pediu para escrever o commit-graph nos checkouts dele e seguir com o F1.\n- Options considered: (a) o motor escreve o commit-graph numa leitura de governança; (b) o `doctor` recomenda e o `doctor --fix` escreve; (c) só documentar.\n- Decision: (b), com o limite de 500 commits.\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\n- Rationale: (a) faria uma leitura escrever no repositório, o que a read view trata como mudança de entrada; (c) não chega a quem não lê o manual; (b) usa o caminho de remediação segura que o POSE já tem. Abaixo de cerca de 500 commits a busca de histórico custa poucos milissegundos.\n- Consequences: um projeto grande vê um passo pendente até rodar a correção.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nTestes do `doctor` com repositórios Git reais; medição já registrada na spec de origem.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./internal/cli -count=1`\n- Scope: pose-mcp\n- Expected: verde\n\n### Execution log\n- Date: 2026-11-16\n- Environment: Linux, Go 1.26, Git 2.56.\n- Notes: o teste monta uma instalação real, cria histórico acima de um limite reduzido e confere o check sem commit-graph (`next`, corrigível), depois da correção (`ok`), com um commit-graph sem filtros (`next`) e com um commit-graph dividido em camadas com filtros (`ok`); abaixo do limite, `ok`. Reprova quando a leitura ignora os chunks BIDX/BDAT, quando ignora a cadeia de camadas e quando a correção não está registrada. O guarda `TestEngineStartsGitOnlyThroughTheRawReader` reprovou a primeira versão, que chamava o Git direto; ela passou a usar `RawHistoryGit`. No checkout do pose-dist do maintainer, com o commit-graph escrito, o check dá `ok`. O `pose doctor` no harne8 passa de 900 s com o binário instalado antes desta spec, e por isso não foi medido lá.\n\n### Results summary\n- Successes: o `doctor` recomenda e escreve o commit-graph; o CI o escreve antes dos gates.\n- Failures: nenhuma.\n- Warnings: o `pose doctor` no harne8 leva mais de 900 s, já antes desta spec (follow-up F1).\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph test:TestDoctorCommitGraphProbesALiteralPath check:doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph-integration\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph test:TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory test:TestDoctorCommitGraphTellsUnbornFromBroken test:TestDoctorCommitGraphNamesDisabledReads\n- R4 [satisfied] O passo `Write the Git commit-graph with changed-path filters` nos dois jobs de `.github/workflows/ci.yml` que fazem checkout do histórico inteiro.\n\n### Known gaps\n- Nenhuma.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n\nNão concluída.\n\n### Residual risks\n\n### Follow-ups\n\n- [open] F1: medir e reduzir o `pose doctor` no harne8, que passa de 900 s em Go, sem processo Git filho, já com o binário anterior a esta spec. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:medium review:2026-11-23)\n\n### Review\n\nRodada 1 (run rrn-237e7d9be5f0c4f5, changes-requested): 4 bloqueadores, corrigidos. F2 a leitura ignorava diretórios de objetos alternativos; F3 preferia a cadeia de camadas ao arquivo único, ao contrário do Git; F4 aceitava cabeçalhos de versão não suportada e arquivos truncados. Os três vinham de reimplementar a leitura do commit-graph; o `doctor` deixou de ler os arquivos e passou a perguntar ao próprio Git: roda `git log -1 -- <path>` com `GIT_TRACE2_EVENT` e procura o evento `bloom`/`statistics` com algum filtro consultado, de modo que vale o grafo que o Git carrega, com as regras dele. O teste cobre um grafo só no alternate (`clone --shared`) e o arquivo único sem filtros ao lado de uma cadeia com filtros (`next`, porque o Git carrega o arquivo único). F5 qualquer erro de leitura suprimia o check; agora só a falta de repositório ou de commit em HEAD o suprime, e as demais falhas viram `warn` com a descrição limitada (`TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory`, com um commit pai apagado).\n\nRodada 2 (run rrn-c7c3feb56ea14783, recusada porque os ids dos achados novos traziam o prefixo de rodada `R2-`; o rascunho declarou F2 a F4 corrigidos e F5 parcial). Os achados, corrigidos com teste que reprova sem a correção: F6 qualquer falha do primeiro `rev-parse` era tratada como \"não se aplica\"; agora só um diretório que não é repositório (`rev-parse --git-dir` com a mensagem do Git, lida no locale C) e um HEAD que não resolve (saída 1 de `rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD`) suprimem o check, e um HEAD cujo commit não pode ser lido, ou um repositório que o Git não abre, é `warn` (`TestDoctorCommitGraphTellsUnbornFromBroken`). F7 o caminho sondado era lido como pathspec, e um nome como `*` mudava a busca; agora a busca roda com `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1` (`TestDoctorCommitGraphProbesALiteralPath`). F8 com `core.commitGraph=false`, `commitGraph.readChangedPaths=false` ou `commitGraph.changedPathsVersion=0`, reescrever o grafo não mudava a resposta; agora o `doctor` reporta `git.commit-graph-reads` em `warn`, sem correção automática, nomeando a configuração, e não recomenda reescrever o grafo (`TestDoctorCommitGraphNamesDisabledReads`).\n\npackage cli\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nfunc doctorCommitGraphFinding(t *testing.T, repo string) doctorFinding {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tvar finding doctorFinding\n\tinDir(t, repo, func() {\n\t\tvar out, errB bytes.Buffer\n\t\tMain([]string{\"doctor\", \"--json\"}, &out, &errB)\n\t\tvar report doctorJSON\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(out.Bytes(), &report); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"invalid JSON: %v\\n%s\", err, out.String())\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, f := range report.Findings {\n\t\t\tif f.Check == \"git.commit-graph\" {\n\t\t\t\tfinding = f\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\tif finding.Check == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"doctor reported no git.commit-graph check\")\n\t}\n\treturn finding\n}\n\nfunc gitInRepo(t *testing.T, repo string, args ...string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tif out, err := exec.Command(\"git\", append([]string{\"-C\", repo}, args...)...).CombinedOutput(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"git %v: %v %s\", args, err, out)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc removeCommitGraph(t *testing.T, repo string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tinfo := filepath.Join(repo, \".git\", \"objects\", \"info\")\n\tfor _, name := range []string{\"commit-graph\", \"commit-graphs\"} {\n\t\tif err := os.RemoveAll(filepath.Join(info, name)); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// Spec pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph: R1 a long history Git walks\n// whole is a next step with a fix; R2 the fix writes the filters and the\n// check turns ok; R3 a short history needs none. The answer is Git's own, so\n// it follows whichever graph Git loads.\nfunc TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph(t *testing.T) {\n\trepo := doctorFixture(t)\n\toriginal := commitGraphHistoryThreshold\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { commitGraphHistoryThreshold = original })\n\tfor i := 0; i < 4; i++ {\n\t\t// Each commit changes a file of its own, so a lookup of another path\n\t\t// has commits the filters can rule out.\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(repo, \"zz-history.txt\"), []byte(strings.Repeat(\"x\", i+1)), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"add\", \"zz-history.txt\")\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"-c\", \"user.name=fixture\", \"-c\", \"user.email=fixture@example.invalid\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\", \"commit\", \"-q\", \"-m\", \"history\")\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\n\tcommitGraphHistoryThreshold = 1 << 30\n\tif f := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); f.Level != \"ok\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a short history is not ok: %+v\", f)\n\t}\n\n\tcommitGraphHistoryThreshold = 3\n\tmissing := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo)\n\tif missing.Level != \"next\" || missing.FixCode != \"git.commit-graph\" || missing.RemediationClass != remediationFixable {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a long history without a commit-graph: %+v\", missing)\n\t}\n\n\tinDir(t, repo, func() {\n\t\tvar out, errB bytes.Buffer\n\t\tMain([]string{\"doctor\", \"--fix\", \"--yes\", \"--only\", \"git.commit-graph\"}, &out, &errB)\n\t})\n\tif fixed := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); fixed.Level != \"ok\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the fix did not satisfy the check: %+v\", fixed)\n\t}\n\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--no-changed-paths\")\n\tif plain := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); plain.Level != \"next\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a commit-graph without changed-path filters is not recommended again: %+v\", plain)\n\t}\n\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--changed-paths\", \"--split\")\n\tif split := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); split.Level != \"ok\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a split commit-graph with changed paths is not recognized: %+v\", split)\n\t}\n\n\t// Review F3: with a single graph and a chain, Git loads the single graph.\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--no-changed-paths\")\n\tif both := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); both.Level != \"next\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unfiltered single graph beside a filtered chain is judged by the chain: %+v\", both)\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--changed-paths\")\n\n\t// Review F2: a repository whose graph lives only in an alternate.\n\tshared := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"shared\")\n\tif out, err := exec.Command(\"git\", \"clone\", \"-q\", \"--shared\", repo, shared).CombinedOutput(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"clone --shared: %v %s\", err, out)\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, shared)\n\tstate, err := readCommitGraphState(shared)\n\tif err != nil || !state.filtersUsed {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a graph in an alternate is not seen: %+v %v\", state, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review F5: a repository whose history cannot be read is a warning, not a\n// silent pass; a root that is not a repository gets no check.\nfunc TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory(t *testing.T) {\n\tif _, err := readCommitGraphState(t.TempDir()); !errors.Is(err, errCommitGraphNotApplicable) {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a directory outside Git: %v, want not applicable\", err)\n\t}\n\trepo := doctorFixture(t)\n\toriginal := commitGraphHistoryThreshold\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { commitGraphHistoryThreshold = original })\n\tcommitGraphHistoryThreshold = 1\n\tcommit := func() {\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"-c\", \"user.name=fixture\", \"-c\", \"user.email=fixture@example.invalid\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\", \"commit\", \"-q\", \"--allow-empty\", \"-m\", \"history\")\n\t}\n\tcommit()\n\tcommit()\n\tout, err := exec.Command(\"git\", \"-C\", repo, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD~1\").Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tparent := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n\tif err := os.Remove(filepath.Join(repo, \".git\", \"objects\", parent[:2], parent[2:])); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the parent commit is not a loose object: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tif f := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); f.Level != \"warn\" || !strings.Contains(f.Message, \"could not be read\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unreadable history: %+v\", f)\n\t}\n}\n\n// longHistoryRepo is an installed repository with commits that each change a\n// file of their own, the threshold lowered under them, and its commit-graph\n// written with changed-path filters.\nfunc longHistoryRepo(t *testing.T, extra func(repo string)) string {\n\tt.Helper()\n\trepo := doctorFixture(t)\n\toriginal := commitGraphHistoryThreshold\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { commitGraphHistoryThreshold = original })\n\tcommitGraphHistoryThreshold = 3\n\tif extra != nil {\n\t\textra(repo)\n\t}\n\tfor i := 0; i < 4; i++ {\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(repo, \"zz-history.txt\"), []byte(strings.Repeat(\"x\", i+1)), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"add\", \"zz-history.txt\")\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"-c\", \"user.name=fixture\", \"-c\", \"user.email=fixture@example.invalid\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\", \"commit\", \"-q\", \"-m\", \"history\")\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--changed-paths\")\n\treturn repo\n}\n\n// Review F6: an unborn HEAD is not applicable, but a repository Git cannot\n// open is reported.\nfunc TestDoctorCommitGraphTellsUnbornFromBroken(t *testing.T) {\n\tunborn := t.TempDir()\n\tgitInRepo(t, unborn, \"init\", \"-q\")\n\tif _, err := readCommitGraphState(unborn); !errors.Is(err, errCommitGraphNotApplicable) {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unborn HEAD: %v, want not applicable\", err)\n\t}\n\tbroken := longHistoryRepo(t, nil)\n\tgitInRepo(t, broken, \"config\", \"core.bare\", \"invalid\")\n\tif _, err := readCommitGraphState(broken); err == nil || errors.Is(err, errCommitGraphNotApplicable) {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a repository with an invalid configuration: %v, want a reported failure\", err)\n\t}\n\theadless := longHistoryRepo(t, nil)\n\tout, err := exec.Command(\"git\", \"-C\", headless, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\thead := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n\tif err := os.Remove(filepath.Join(headless, \".git\", \"objects\", head[:2], head[2:])); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the HEAD commit is not a loose object: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, headless)\n\tif _, err := readCommitGraphState(headless); err == nil || errors.Is(err, errCommitGraphNotApplicable) {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a HEAD whose commit is missing: %v, want a reported failure\", err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review F7: a first file whose name is pathspec syntax is looked up as the\n// literal name, so the filters still answer.\nfunc TestDoctorCommitGraphProbesALiteralPath(t *testing.T) {\n\trepo := longHistoryRepo(t, func(repo string) {\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(repo, \"*\"), []byte(\"star\\n\"), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"add\", \"--\", \"*\")\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"-c\", \"user.name=fixture\", \"-c\", \"user.email=fixture@example.invalid\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\", \"commit\", \"-q\", \"-m\", \"star\")\n\t})\n\tout, err := exec.Command(\"git\", \"-C\", repo, \"ls-tree\", \"-r\", \"--name-only\", \"HEAD\").Output()\n\tif err != nil || !strings.HasPrefix(string(out), \"*\\n\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the fixture's first path is not '*': %q %v\", out, err)\n\t}\n\tstate, err := readCommitGraphState(repo)\n\tif err != nil || !state.filtersUsed {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a filtered graph probed through a pathspec-like name: %+v %v\", state, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review F8: configuration that keeps Git from reading the filters is a\n// warning that names it, not a graph rewrite that cannot help.\nfunc TestDoctorCommitGraphNamesDisabledReads(t *testing.T) {\n\trepo := longHistoryRepo(t, nil)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"config\", \"commitGraph.changedPathsVersion\", \"0\")\n\tvar reads doctorFinding\n\tinDir(t, repo, func() {\n\t\tvar out, errB bytes.Buffer\n\t\tMain([]string{\"doctor\", \"--json\"}, &out, &errB)\n\t\tvar report doctorJSON\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(out.Bytes(), &report); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"invalid JSON: %v\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, f := range report.Findings {\n\t\t\tif f.Check == \"git.commit-graph\" {\n\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"the graph rewrite is recommended although configuration disables reading it: %+v\", f)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif f.Check == \"git.commit-graph-reads\" {\n\t\t\t\treads = f\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\tif reads.Level != \"warn\" || !strings.Contains(reads.Message, \"commitGraph.changedPathsVersion=0\") || reads.FixCode != \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"disabled filter reads: %+v\", reads)\n\t}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
